#
# A gate whose first branch cannot be answered and whose second one can, guarding a body that fails.
#
# `Enabled` is a boolean, so `!EMPTY` on it has no answer. `Name == "keep"` holds. A disjunction asks
# for at least one satisfied branch, and it has one, so the gate opens and `MustBeTrue == true` runs
# and fails.
#
# That is not what happened before the clause path carried `Outcome`. The undecidable branch left the
# evaluator as an error, which returned from the conjunction immediately -- so the second branch was
# never evaluated, the rule failed closed on its condition, and the violation inside the body went
# unreported. The exit code was the same either way, which is why this needs an output assertion
# rather than a status one: the improvement is that the finding appears, not that the file fails.
#
# `Outcome::or` absorbs only `Satisfied`, so this is not a general relaxation. A disjunction whose
# branches are all undecidable is still undecidable, and a gate that cannot be decided still fails
# its rule closed -- `unevaluatable_gate_guarding_a_violation.guard` is that case.
#
rule guarded when Enabled !EMPTY or Name == "keep" {
    MustBeTrue == true
}
